        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: What is the population of East Brooklyn?\n\nAnswer A: 5,000\n  about: East Brooklyn\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 2000\n  kind: factual\n  attributed to: United States Census Bureau\n  independent sources: 2\n  evidence: \"There were 5,000 people, 668 households, and 378 families residing in the CDP.\" [document 2]; \"The population was 5,000 at the 2000 census.\" [document 3]\n\nAnswer B: 106\n  about: East Brooklyn\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 2010\n  kind: factual\n  attributed to: the 2010 census\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"The population was 106 at the 2010 census.\" [document 4]\n\nAnswer C: 80\n  about: East Brooklyn\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 2020\n  kind: factual\n  attributed to: U.S. Decennial Census\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"[ 2020 census [ 3 ] there were 80 people , 32 households]\" [document 5]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: East Brooklyn, Connecticut East Brooklyn is a census-designated place located within the town of Brooklyn in Windham County, Connecticut. It is the portion of the Danielson urban cluster within the town of Brooklyn. The population was 1,473 at the 2000 census. US 6 runs through the town and severs the town. The portion of the CDP near the Quinebaug River is known as the Quinebaug Mill-Quebec Square Historic District. According to the United States Census Bureau, the CDP has a total area of 4.3 km² (1.6 mi²). 4.2 km² (1.6 mi²) of it is land and 0.1 km² (0.04 mi²)\nDocument 2: There were 5,000 people, 668 households, and 378 families residing in the CDP. The population density was 1,189.9 people/km² (3,082 people/sq mi). There were 690 housing units at an average density of 163.4 units/km² (423 units/sq mi). The racial makeup of the CDP was 94.30% White, 2.04% African American, 0.81% Native American, 0.75% Asian, 0.41% from other races, and 1.70% from two or more races. Hispanic or Latino of any race were 1.83% of the population.\nDocument 3: East Brooklyn, Connecticut East Brooklyn is a census-designated place located within the town of Brooklyn in Windham County, Connecticut. It is the portion of the Danielson urban cluster within the town of Brooklyn. The population was 5,000 at the 2000 census. US 6 runs through the town and severs the town. The portion of the CDP near the Quinebaug River is known as the Quinebaug Mill-Quebec Square Historic District. According to the United States Census Bureau, the CDP has a total area of 4.3 km² (1.6 mi²). 4.2 km² (1.6 mi²) of it is land and 0.1 km² (0.04 mi²) of it is water.\nDocument 4: East Brooklyn, Illinois East Brooklyn is a village in Grundy County, Illinois, United States. The population was 106 at the 2010 census. East Brooklyn is located at (41.172464, -88.265729). According to the 2010 census, East Brooklyn has a total area of , of which (or 98.04%) is land and (or 1.96%) is water. As of the census of 2000, there were 123 people, 46 households, and 33 families residing in the village. The population density was 2,369.4 people per square mile (949.8/km²). There were 51 housing units at an average density of 982.4 per square mile (393.8/km²). The racial makeup\nDocument 5: has a total area of 0.05 square miles ( 0.13 km 2 ) , all land . [ 4 ] Demographics [ edit ] Historical population Census Pop . Note % ± 1910 446 — 1920 204 −54.3 % 1930 79 −61.3 % 1940 68 −13.9 % 1950 65 −4.4 % 1960 68 4.6 % 1970 72 5.9 % 1980 84 16.7 % 1990 80 −4.8 % 2000 123 53.8 % 2010 106 −13.8 % 2020 80 −24.5 % U.S. Decennial Census [ 5 ] As of the 2020 census [ 3 ] there were 80 people , 32 households\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
